  "narrative": "The metabolomics profiling reveals a pronounced perturbation in **Glycine, serine and threonine metabolism (KEGG map00260)** — the top-ranking KEGG pathway with an FDR near zero and a remarkable fold enrichment of ~603. This pathway is a central hub for one-carbon metabolism, connecting amino acid catabolism, methylation chemistry, and nucleotide biosynthesis. Seven of the eleven input compounds map to this pathway, making it the dominant signal in the dataset.\n\nThe key driver metabolites driving this enrichment include **phosphoserine (C01005)**, **sarcosine (C00213)**, **S-adenosylmethionine (SAM, C00019)**, and **L-cystathionine (C02291)**, all of which were confirmed as pathway members. **Phosphoserine** sits at the entry point of serine biosynthesis from glycolytic intermediates, while **sarcosine** is an intermediate in the dimethylglycine–glycine axis. **SAM**, the universal methyl donor, bridges one-carbon metabolism to the trans-sulfuration pathway via its relationship to **L-cystathionine**, which we verified lies downstream of SAM through a 3-hop KEGG reaction trajectory (SAM → C00021 → C00155 → cystathionine). Similarly, phosphoserine is connected to sarcosine through a 3-hop route (phosphoserine → C00065 → C00037 → sarcosine), confirming tight metabolic adjacency within this network.\n\nThe **trans-sulfuration pathway (hsa00270)** is the second major thread — **(R)-lipoic acid (C16241)** participates as a cofactor in the re-methylation steps linking homocysteine to methionine, directly feeding into SAM synthesis. **Choline (C00114)** contributes methyl groups via the betaine–dimethylglycine shunt, reinforcing the one-carbon pool. **Adenosine monophosphate (C00020)** reflects heightened demand for purine biosynthesis, consistent with increased methyltransferase activity consuming SAM.\n\nThe convergence on one-carbon metabolism suggests the treatment likely induces a state of intensified methyl-group flux — potentially through altered methionine synthase or betaine–homocysteine methyltransferase activity — with downstream effects on methylation capacity, antioxidant defense, and nucleotide synthesis. Dysregulation of this network is implicated in neural tube defects, fatty liver, and cancer progression, making these metabolites actionable biomarkers for downstream mechanistic follow-up.",
